<article class="env-lemma" id="0DUN">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DUN">Lemma <span data-tag="0DUN">106.13.3</span>.</a> Let the algebraic stack $\mathcal{X}$ be well-nigh affine. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $\mathcal{X}$ is an algebraic space, then it is affine. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $\mathcal{X}' \to \mathcal{X}$ is an affine morphism of algebraic stacks, then $\mathcal{X}'$ is well-nigh affine. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Part (1) follows from immediately from Limits of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/07VS" data-tag="07VS">70.15.1</a>. However, this is overkill, since (1) also follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0DUM" data-tag="0DUM">106.13.2</a> combined with Groupoids, Proposition <a href="/tag/03BM" data-tag="03BM">39.23.9</a>. 
    <p>
      To prove (2) we choose an affine scheme $U$ and a surjective, flat, finite, and finitely presented morphism $U \to \mathcal{X}$. Then $U' = \mathcal{X}' \times _\mathcal {X} U$ admits an affine morphism to $U$ (Morphisms of Stacks, Lemma <a href="/tag/0CHR" data-tag="0CHR">101.9.2</a>). Therefore $U'$ is an affine scheme. Of course $U' \to \mathcal{X}'$ is surjective, flat, finite, and finitely presented as a base change of $U \to \mathcal{X}$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>